# Delegation Guide

This project is driven by a hosted orchestrator (you) with a `delegate_to_local`
MCP tool available for offloading bounded, well-defined sub-tasks to a local
or remote delegate worker. This guide is the fuller version of the policy
summarized in that tool's description -- read it once per session, then rely
on the tool description's shorter reminder for the mechanical "use for X not
Y" call.

The policy here is a static, predefined guide (not a dynamic router): a
project's `josu.toml` maps task types to a ranked fallback chain of delegate
candidates, and this document tells you which task types are worth
delegating in the first place. Sensible defaults are listed below; a
project's `josu.toml` can override them, including forcing a specific task
type to stay with you regardless of what these defaults suggest.

## Delegable task types

These are bounded enough that a smaller, cheaper delegate model produces a
usable result, and any inaccuracy is easy for you to spot-check against the
`caveats` field the tool returns:

- **File/directory summarization** -- describing what a file or directory
  contains, without needing to reason about how it fits into the wider
  system.
- **Boilerplate/scaffolding** -- generating code against an established,
  already-present pattern in the codebase (a new file that mirrors an
  existing one's shape, a new test file skeleton, config boilerplate).
- **Simple search/extraction** -- finding and pulling out information that's
  explicitly present in the codebase (e.g. "list every place X is imported"),
  not synthesis or judgment calls about what's relevant.
- **Pattern-matched test generation** -- writing tests that follow an
  existing test file's structure and conventions for a well-understood
  change.

## Stays with you (the hosted orchestrator)

Do not delegate these, even if a delegate candidate is configured and
available:

- **Complex refactors** -- anything touching multiple files where the
  correctness of the change depends on understanding relationships across
  the codebase, not just the local pattern of one file.
- **Architecture decisions** -- choices that shape how future work will be
  done, not just what one file contains right now.
- **Ambiguous requirements** -- anything where the task itself needs
  interpretation or clarification before it can be executed; a delegate
  candidate can't ask a clarifying question the way you can reason through
  ambiguity.
- **Security-sensitive changes** -- auth, secrets handling, permission
  checks, anything where a subtly wrong delegate output is a real risk
  rather than a cosmetic one.

A project's `josu.toml` can mark a specific task type with `stays_hosted =
true` in its `[[delegation.chains]]` entry. When set, that task type must
stay with you even if it would otherwise look delegable by the categories
above, and even if candidates are listed for it in config -- this is the
developer's explicit override of the guide's default, and it takes
precedence over everything else in this document.

## Fallback chains and free-local-first ordering

Each delegable task type resolves to a ranked list of candidates, tried in
order: free/local candidates are preferred before any paid remote one by
default, and a remote candidate is only ever eligible at all when the
project has explicitly opted in (`allow_remote = true` in `josu.toml`) --
absent that flag, remote candidates are excluded from every chain regardless
of how they're configured. You don't need to reason about this ordering
yourself; it happens before your call reaches a candidate. What you do need
to know is what happens when the whole chain is exhausted.

## Chain-exhausted fallback

If a `delegate_to_local` call returns a chain-exhausted error, every
candidate configured for that task type was tried and none of them
succeeded. In that case:

- **Perform the sub-task yourself.** Do not retry the same delegation call
  again -- the chain is exhausted, not rate-limited or transiently down, and
  retrying it wastes a turn without a different outcome.
- **Do not leave the sub-task incomplete.** The point of delegation is to
  offload work you'd otherwise do directly; when delegation fails
  end-to-end, fall back to doing the work directly rather than skipping it
  or reporting it as blocked.

This is the same instruction the tool description gives in short form; this
document is the explanation of why it's the right move, not a different
policy.

## Proactive checks are a separate, local-only path

Commit- and save-triggered proactive checks (re-evaluating the graph for
issues) use a distinct `proactive_check` category that is restricted to
local candidates by construction -- it never falls through to a remote
candidate, even if `allow_remote` is set and even if a remote candidate is
ranked first for other task types. If no local candidate is available when
a proactive check would run, the check is skipped for that event rather than
escalated to a remote candidate. You are only woken up if a proactive check
surfaces something serious; routine proactive-check activity does not
involve you at all.
